  "reason": "1) Evidence/uncertainty: No experiments have been run, so we have zero information about any design dimension. The most fundamental question for a multimodal cellular profiling study is whether the basic multimodal pipeline (RNA + image) is viable and what performance level to expect. All other decisions (encoder choice, profile setting, aggregation, fusion strategy) are secondary to this core question. 2) What this tests: config_016 (multimodal, singleprofile, pca_n2000, resnet50, concat) is the simplest end-to-end multimodal configuration. It tests whether combining a basic RNA representation (PCA) with a standard image backbone (ResNet50) via the simplest fusion (concat) yields a working, competitive model. It establishes a reference performance for the multimodal paradigm. 3) Why it is the best use of the first budget: Starting at the center of the configuration space with the most straightforward choices avoids confounding multiple complex design decisions. If performance is strong, it validates the multimodal direction and gives a clear baseline to exploit (better encoders, multiprofile, transformer aggregation, weighted fusion). If performance is weak, the diagnosis is cleaner—since each component is a simple, well-understood default, we can isolate whether the bottleneck is the RNA encoder, the image encoder, the fusion strategy, or the lack of multiprofile aggregation. This single experiment thus reduces the most uncertainty per unit of budget and sets the stage for targeted follow-up experiments."
